      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\left(- y - e - 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(- y - 1 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following are equilibrium solutions? Select all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An equilibrium is a constant function $y(t)=c$. Set $dy/dt=0$ and test the resulting equation.\n\nFirst keep the original domain: $- y - 1>0$.\n\nWe need\n\n$$\nf(c) = \\left(- c - e - 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(- c - 1 \\right)}=0.\n$$\n\n- For $c=-3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) &=\\left(- e + 2\\right) \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) &=\\left(- e + 3\\right) \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=-2$: Use $\\ln{\\left(1 \\right)}=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) &=\\left(- e + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(1 \\right)} \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=0$, $- c - 1=-1$ violates the original domain, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\nSelect $y=-2$.",
